00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:59.220
$1,500 share position. What actually happened is that the bid-ask spread on that thinly traded name

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:05.420
widened from 4 cents to 22 cents in the first 45 minutes after the news. Market makers pull back

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:10.980
liquidity when geopolitical or policy headlines create uncertainty. They widen spreads to protect

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:18.280
themselves. On the TSX, this effect is magnified for names with average daily volume under 250,000 shares.

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:25.480
The pro tip most retail investors miss is to always check the spread and 20 day average volume before

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:31.860
hitting buy. Professionals use limit orders placed between the bid and ask rather than chasing momentum.

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:36.940
The biggest mistake is treating all stocks as equally liquid during news events.

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:42.360
Instead, always verify real-time depth and set your limit order at or inside the current spread

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:47.540
before entering. This discipline of waiting for proper conditions is exactly why we're sitting on

